  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes neuromodulation approaches used at different points along the gastrointestinal tract and the conditions they are intended to treat. A simplified digestive system is shown with the liver, stomach, small intestine, colon, and external anal sphincter, alongside colored symbols marking where nerves are stimulated, blocked, or where gastric electrical stimulation is applied.\n\nThe main message is that different neural targets along the vagal, sympathetic, and sacral pathways can influence specific digestive symptoms or diseases. Near the upper gut, vagal nerve stimulation is linked to inflammatory bowel disease and ileus, while vagal block is associated with obesity treatment. The stomach is also a target for gastric electrical stimulation, used for nausea, discomfort related to gastroparesis, and obesity. Lower in the abdomen, sympathetic stimulation appears aimed at inflammatory bowel disease, and tibial nerve stimulation is indicated for constipation. At the pelvic outlet, sacral nerve stimulation is connected with fecal incontinence, constipation, and inflammation.\n\nArrows indicate the direction of nerve conduction being targeted, showing whether therapies act along upward or downward neural signaling. Overall, the diagram presents the gut as a system that can be therapeutically modulated through several distinct nerve circuits.",
